window.LAB_ITEMS = {"version":1,"lab":3,"course":"Phonetics","title":"Where Sounds Are Made","zodiac":{"index":3,"animal":"tiger","label":"Tiger"},"unit":{"one":"Trail","many":"trails"},"shuffle_items":true,"lede":"You will find the articulators that make each sound on a map of the mouth, work out which sound a picture shows from where and how it is made, and build ten words out of their sounds.","grading":{"total":30,"full_threshold":20,"full_score":100,"partial_score":50,"zero_score":0,"full_points":2,"partial_points":1,"alt_credit":0.5,"round":"up"},"legs":[{"id":"af","n":1,"title":"Articulator Finder","accent":"amber","subtitle":"Tap the articulators that make each sound","concept":"Consonants are made by bringing one part of the mouth or throat against, or close to, another. The parts that do this are the <b>articulators</b>: the lips, the upper teeth, the alveolar ridge just behind them, the postalveolar area just behind the ridge, the hard palate, the velum (soft palate) at the back, the tongue's tip, blade and back, and the vocal folds. Most consonants use two: a part that moves, usually the tongue, and the place it moves to. A few, like /b/, use just the lips.","audio_note":"<b>Tap the sound to hear it.</b> A consonant is hard to hear on its own, so most are recorded with a vowel after: /b/ plays as <i>ba</i>. Listen to the front of the syllable.","prompt":"Tap every articulator this sound uses, then press Check.","regions":[{"v":"lips","label":"Lips"},{"v":"teeth","label":"Upper teeth"},{"v":"alveolar","label":"Alveolar ridge"},{"v":"postalv","label":"Postalveolar area"},{"v":"palate","label":"Hard palate"},{"v":"velum","label":"Velum"},{"v":"tip","label":"Tongue tip"},{"v":"blade","label":"Tongue blade"},{"v":"back","label":"Tongue back"},{"v":"glottis","label":"Vocal folds"}],"items":[{"id":"af01","type":"multispot","ipa":"b","answer":"lips","explain":"/b/ is made at the lips and nowhere else: the two lips close, air builds up behind them, and the sound is the release. A sound made with both lips is called <b>bilabial</b>.","miss":{"lips.teeth":"Lip against teeth is the /f/ and /v/ position. For /b/ the upper lip meets the lower lip, and the teeth stay out of it.","alveolar.tip":"That is the /t/ and /d/ spot. Say <i>bob</i> slowly and feel where it starts: for /b/ the lips do all the work."},"also":["glottis.lips"],"accept":{},"note":null,"quiet":["glottis.lips"]},{"id":"af02","type":"multispot","ipa":"t","answer":"alveolar.tip","accept":{"alveolar":"Right place, but a place needs something to touch it. The tongue <b>tip</b> is what rises to the alveolar ridge for /t/; both parts are the answer.","tip":"Right mover. The tip has to land somewhere, and for /t/ it lands on the <b>alveolar ridge</b>; both parts are the answer.","alveolar.glottis.tip":"Every part in the mouth is right. The slip is the <b>vocal folds</b>: /t/ is <b>voiceless</b>, so they stay open and still while it is made. Put a hand on your throat and say it: no buzz."},"explain":"/t/ is an <b>alveolar</b> sound: the tongue tip presses against the alveolar ridge, the bony bump just behind the upper front teeth, and blocks the air completely before letting it go.","miss":{"blade.postalv":"One step too far back. The area just behind the ridge is where /ʃ/ is made. /t/ is on the ridge itself, with the tip.","teeth.tip":"Tip to the teeth is /θ/ and /ð/, as in <i>think</i>. For /t/ the tip lands just behind the teeth, on the ridge."},"also":[],"note":null,"quiet":[]},{"id":"af03","type":"multispot","ipa":"w","answer":"lips","also":["back.lips","back.lips.velum","glottis.lips","back.glottis.lips","back.glottis.lips.velum"],"explain":"/w/ is first of all a lip sound: both lips round tightly, as for whistling, and the air glides through the small opening without ever being blocked. At the same time the back of the tongue rises toward the velum, which is why fuller descriptions call /w/ <b>labial-velar</b>. Lips alone, or lips plus tongue back (with or without the velum), all count here.","miss":{"lips.teeth":"Lower lip on the upper teeth is /f/ and /v/. For /w/ the lips round together and the teeth are not involved.","back.velum":"That is the /k/ and /g/ position, and the back of the tongue does rise for /w/, but the lips are the part that defines it. Say <i>we</i> in a mirror: the lips are what move.","back":"The back of the tongue does rise a little for /w/, but on its own that is not the sound. What defines /w/ is the tight rounding of the lips."},"accept":{},"note":null,"quiet":["back.lips.velum","glottis.lips","back.glottis.lips","back.glottis.lips.velum"]},{"id":"af04","type":"multispot","ipa":"g","answer":"back.velum","accept":{"velum":"Right place. The velum needs something to press against it, and for /g/ that is the <b>back of the tongue</b>; both parts are the answer.","back":"Right mover. The back of the tongue rises for /g/, and what it presses against is the <b>velum</b>; both parts are the answer.","glottis.velum":"Right place. The velum needs something to press against it, and for /g/ that is the <b>back of the tongue</b>; both parts are the answer.","back.glottis":"Right mover. The back of the tongue rises for /g/, and what it presses against is the <b>velum</b>; both parts are the answer."},"explain":"/g/ is a <b>velar</b> sound: the back of the tongue humps up against the velum, the soft part of the roof of the mouth, and seals the air off there before releasing it. Say <i>go</i> and feel how far back it happens.","miss":{"back.palate":"Close, but the hard palate is the bony front part of the roof. /g/ is made farther back, where the roof turns soft: the velum.","alveolar.tip":"That is the front of the mouth, where /d/ is made. /g/ is made at the very back, with the back of the tongue."},"also":["back.glottis.velum"],"note":null,"quiet":["back.glottis.velum"]},{"id":"af05","type":"multispot","ipa":"k","answer":"back.velum","accept":{"velum":"Right place; add the mover. It is the <b>back of the tongue</b> that rises to the velum for /k/; both parts are the answer.","back":"Right mover; add the place. For /k/ the back of the tongue presses against the <b>velum</b>; both parts are the answer.","back.glottis.velum":"Every part in the mouth is right. The slip is the <b>vocal folds</b>: /k/ is <b>voiceless</b>, so they stay open and still while it is made. Put a hand on your throat and say it: no buzz."},"explain":"/k/ is made in exactly the same place as /g/: the back of the tongue against the velum, so it is <b>velar</b> too. The two differ only in the voice: the vocal folds vibrate for /g/ and stay still for /k/.","miss":{"back.palate":"The hard palate is farther forward than /k/ goes. Say <i>key</i>, then <i>car</i>: the seal is at the soft back of the roof, the velum."},"also":[],"note":null,"quiet":[]},{"id":"af06","type":"multispot","ipa":"ð","answer":"teeth.tip","also":["teeth","glottis.teeth.tip","glottis.teeth"],"accept":{"tip":"Right mover. For /ð/ the tip goes all the way forward to the <b>upper teeth</b>, not to the ridge behind them; the teeth are the place that names the sound.","glottis.tip":"Right mover. For /ð/ the tip goes all the way forward to the <b>upper teeth</b>, not to the ridge behind them; the teeth are the place that names the sound."},"explain":"/ð/, the first sound of <i>this</i>, is an <b>interdental</b> sound: the tongue tip touches the edge of the upper front teeth, or slips between the teeth, and air hisses through the gap. The teeth are the place, the tip is what reaches them; naming the teeth alone, or teeth plus tongue tip, both count here.","miss":{"alveolar.tip":"That is one step back, on the ridge, where /d/ and /z/ are made. For /ð/ the tip comes all the way forward to the teeth.","lips.teeth":"Lip against teeth is /f/ and /v/. The /ð/ contact is tongue against teeth."},"note":null,"quiet":["glottis.teeth.tip","glottis.teeth"]},{"id":"af07","type":"multispot","ipa":"z","answer":"alveolar.tip","accept":{"alveolar":"Right place; add the mover. The <b>tongue tip</b> rises toward the ridge for /z/; both parts are the answer.","tip":"Right mover; add the place. For /z/ the tip rises to the <b>alveolar ridge</b>; both parts are the answer.","alveolar.glottis":"Right place; add the mover. The <b>tongue tip</b> rises toward the ridge for /z/; both parts are the answer.","glottis.tip":"Right mover; add the place. For /z/ the tip rises to the <b>alveolar ridge</b>; both parts are the answer."},"explain":"/z/ is <b>alveolar</b>, made in the same spot as /t/: tongue tip up to the alveolar ridge. But where /t/ seals the air off, /z/ leaves a narrow groove for it to hiss through.","miss":{"blade.postalv":"A little too far back: that is the /ʃ/ and /ʒ/ position. /z/ is made at the ridge itself.","teeth.tip":"Tip to the teeth gives /ð/, as in <i>this</i>. /z/ sits just behind the teeth, on the ridge."},"also":["alveolar.glottis.tip"],"note":null,"quiet":["alveolar.glottis.tip"]},{"id":"af08","type":"multispot","ipa":"ʃ","answer":"blade.postalv","accept":{"postalv":"Right place; add the mover. It is the <b>tongue blade</b> that rises there for /ʃ/. Both parts are the answer.","blade":"Right mover; add the place. The blade rises to the <b>postalveolar area</b>, just behind the ridge. Both parts are the answer.","blade.glottis.postalv":"Every part in the mouth is right. The slip is the <b>vocal folds</b>: /ʃ/ is <b>voiceless</b>, so they stay open and still while it is made. Put a hand on your throat and say it: no buzz."},"explain":"/ʃ/, as in <i>shoe</i>, is <b>postalveolar</b>: the tongue blade, the flat part just behind the tip, rises toward the roof of the mouth just behind the alveolar ridge, and air rushes through the narrow gap.","miss":{"alveolar.tip":"That is the /s/ position. Say <i>see</i> then <i>she</i>: for <i>she</i> the tongue pulls back a little and its blade, not its tip, does the work."},"also":[],"note":null,"quiet":[]},{"id":"af09","type":"multispot","ipa":"n","answer":"alveolar.tip","accept":{"alveolar":"Right place; add the mover. The <b>tongue tip</b> is what touches the ridge for /n/; both parts are the answer.","tip":"Right mover; add the place. For /n/ the tip lands on the <b>alveolar ridge</b>; both parts are the answer.","alveolar.glottis":"Right place; add the mover. The <b>tongue tip</b> is what touches the ridge for /n/; both parts are the answer.","glottis.tip":"Right mover; add the place. For /n/ the tip lands on the <b>alveolar ridge</b>; both parts are the answer."},"explain":"/n/ is <b>alveolar</b> too: tongue tip on the alveolar ridge, sealing the mouth exactly as for /t/ and /d/. The air escapes through the nose instead, which is why holding your nose spoils it.","miss":{"back.velum":"Back of the tongue against the velum gives the other nasal, /ŋ/, as at the end of <i>sing</i>. /n/ is made at the front, tip on the ridge.","lips":"Lips together gives the nasal /m/. For /n/ the lips stay open and the tongue tip does the sealing.","alveolar.tip.velum":"The velum does lower for /n/, to open the way into the nose, but the place where /n/ is made is the tongue tip on the alveolar ridge.","alveolar.glottis.tip.velum":"The velum does lower for /n/, to open the way into the nose, but the place where /n/ is made is the tongue tip on the alveolar ridge."},"also":["alveolar.glottis.tip"],"note":null,"quiet":["alveolar.glottis.tip"]},{"id":"af10","type":"multispot","ipa":"dʒ","answer":"blade.postalv","accept":{"postalv":"Right place; add the mover. The <b>tongue blade</b> makes the contact for /dʒ/; both parts are the answer.","blade":"Right mover; add the place. The blade goes to the <b>postalveolar area</b>, just behind the ridge, the same spot as /ʃ/; both parts are the answer.","glottis.postalv":"Right place; add the mover. The <b>tongue blade</b> makes the contact for /dʒ/; both parts are the answer.","blade.glottis":"Right mover; add the place. The blade goes to the <b>postalveolar area</b>, just behind the ridge, the same spot as /ʃ/; both parts are the answer."},"explain":"/dʒ/, the first sound of <i>jump</i>, is <b>postalveolar</b>: tongue blade up just behind the alveolar ridge, the same place as /ʃ/. It starts with a full closure like /d/ and releases into a /ʒ/-like hiss.","miss":{"alveolar.tip":"That is the /d/ spot, and /dʒ/ does start like a /d/, but pulled back a step: blade, not tip, just behind the ridge.","back.velum":"The velum is the /g/ and /k/ position. /dʒ/ is made much farther forward, just behind the ridge."},"also":["blade.glottis.postalv"],"note":null,"quiet":["blade.glottis.postalv"]}]},{"id":"sd","n":2,"title":"Sound Detective","accent":"indigo","subtitle":"Work out which sound the picture shows","concept":"A consonant can be described by three facts: <b>where</b> it is made, which articulators touch or come close; <b>how</b> it is made, whether the air is blocked, squeezed through a gap, or sent through the nose; and whether the <b>vocal folds vibrate</b> while it happens. Given those three facts, there is only one sound it can be.","prompt":"Read the picture, then tap the sound it shows.","tiles":[{"ipa":"p","slug":"p"},{"ipa":"ʒ","slug":"zh"},{"ipa":"d","slug":"d"},{"ipa":"s","slug":"s"},{"ipa":"f","slug":"f"},{"ipa":"θ","slug":"th"},{"ipa":"ŋ","slug":"ng"},{"ipa":"j","slug":"y"},{"ipa":"m","slug":"m"},{"ipa":"h","slug":"h"}],"items":[{"id":"sd01","type":"pick","answer":"p","panel":{"show":["lips"],"flow":"stop","place":"Both lips pressed together","manner":"Air is blocked completely, then released in a burst","voiced":false},"explain":"Lips together, air fully blocked, no voice: that is /p/. Its voiced twin /b/ is made in exactly the same place; the silent vocal folds are what make this one /p/.","miss":{"m":"/m/ is made with the lips too, but for /m/ the air flows out through the nose and the vocal folds vibrate. This panel shows a full block and no voice.","f":"/f/ is lip against teeth with air hissing through. Here both lips meet and the air is stopped, not hissed."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd02","type":"pick","answer":"zh","panel":{"show":["blade","postalv"],"flow":"fric","place":"Tongue blade raised just behind the alveolar ridge","manner":"A narrow gap; air rushes through with friction","voiced":true},"accept":{"s":"You read the hiss correctly. Two things separate them: /ʒ/ is <b>voiced</b>, and it is made a step farther back, with the tongue blade just behind the ridge rather than the tip at it."},"explain":"Blade just behind the ridge, air hissing through, and the voice on: that is /ʒ/, the middle sound of <i>measure</i>. It is the voiced twin of /ʃ/.","miss":{"d":"/d/ is made a little farther forward, tip on the ridge, and it blocks the air completely. This panel shows a gap with air rushing through, not a block.","y":"/j/ is made farther back, with the front of the tongue near the hard palate, and the gap is wide enough that there is no friction at all."},"also":[],"note":null},{"id":"sd03","type":"pick","answer":"d","panel":{"show":["tip","alveolar"],"flow":"stop","place":"Tongue tip against the alveolar ridge","manner":"Air is blocked completely, then released in a burst","voiced":true},"explain":"Tip on the ridge, a full block, and the voice on: that is /d/. Made in the same place as /t/; the vibrating vocal folds are what make it /d/.","miss":{"s":"/s/ is made in the same place, but with a narrow groove for the air to hiss through, and without voice. This panel shows a full block with voice.","ng":"/ŋ/ is made at the back of the mouth, tongue back against the velum, with air through the nose. This contact is at the front, on the ridge."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd04","type":"pick","answer":"s","panel":{"show":["tip","alveolar"],"flow":"fric","place":"Tongue tip raised toward the alveolar ridge","manner":"A narrow groove; air hisses through","voiced":false},"accept":{"zh":"You read the hiss correctly. But /s/ has <b>no voice</b>, and it is made a step farther forward than /ʒ/, with the tongue tip at the ridge."},"explain":"Tip toward the ridge, a narrow groove, air hissing, no voice: that is /s/. Same place as /d/, but the air squeezes through instead of being blocked.","miss":{"th":"/θ/ hisses too, but the tip is between the teeth, farther forward than the ridge shown here.","d":"/d/ is made in the same place, but it blocks the air completely and is voiced. This panel shows a hiss with the voice off."},"also":[],"note":null},{"id":"sd05","type":"pick","answer":"f","panel":{"show":["lips","teeth"],"flow":"fric","place":"Lower lip against the upper teeth","manner":"A narrow gap; air hisses through","voiced":false},"explain":"Lower lip touching the upper teeth, air hissing through, no voice: that is /f/, a <b>labiodental</b> sound. Its voiced twin is /v/.","miss":{"p":"/p/ uses both lips together and blocks the air. Here one lip touches the teeth and the air keeps flowing.","th":"/θ/ is tongue tip between the teeth. Here it is the lip at the teeth, not the tongue."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd06","type":"pick","answer":"th","panel":{"show":["tip","teeth"],"flow":"fric","place":"Tongue tip between the teeth","manner":"A narrow gap; air hisses through","voiced":false},"explain":"Tongue tip at the teeth, air hissing, no voice: that is /θ/, the first sound of <i>think</i>. Its voiced twin /ð/ is made in the same place with the voice on.","miss":{"s":"/s/ is made a step farther back, tip toward the ridge, not between the teeth.","f":"/f/ is lip against teeth. Here it is the tongue tip at the teeth."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd07","type":"pick","answer":"ng","panel":{"show":["back","velum"],"flow":"nasal","place":"Back of the tongue against the velum","manner":"The mouth is sealed; air flows out through the nose","voiced":true},"explain":"Tongue back against the velum, mouth sealed, air through the nose, voice on: that is /ŋ/, the last sound of <i>sing</i>. It is the nasal made in the /k/ and /g/ place.","miss":{"m":"/m/ is a nasal too, but sealed at the lips. This seal is at the back, tongue against the velum.","d":"/d/ is at the front, tip on the ridge, and its air leaves through the mouth. This panel shows the back of the mouth and air through the nose."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd08","type":"pick","answer":"y","panel":{"show":["blade","palate"],"flow":"approx","place":"Front of the tongue raised toward the hard palate","manner":"A wide gap; air glides through without friction","voiced":true},"explain":"Tongue front toward the hard palate, a gap too wide for any hiss, voice on: that is /j/, the first sound of <i>yes</i>. Sounds that narrow the mouth without friction are called <b>approximants</b>.","miss":{"zh":"/ʒ/ is close by, but its gap is narrow enough to hiss. This panel shows a wide gap and smooth airflow.","ng":"/ŋ/ seals the mouth at the velum and sends air through the nose. Here the mouth stays open."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd09","type":"pick","answer":"m","panel":{"show":["lips"],"flow":"nasal","place":"Both lips pressed together","manner":"The mouth is sealed; air flows out through the nose","voiced":true},"explain":"Lips together, air through the nose, voice on: that is /m/. The lips seal as for /p/ and /b/, but the velum lowers, opening the way into the nose.","miss":{"p":"/p/ seals the lips too, but the air is blocked and released through the mouth, with no voice. This panel shows air through the nose and the voice on.","ng":"/ŋ/ is the nasal made at the back of the mouth. This seal is at the lips."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"sd10","type":"pick","answer":"h","panel":{"show":["glottis"],"flow":"glottal","place":"Nothing in the mouth is touching","manner":"Air rushes through the open vocal folds","voiced":false},"explain":"No contact anywhere in the mouth, air just rushing through the open vocal folds: that is /h/, a <b>glottal</b> sound. The mouth is already shaped for whatever vowel comes next.","miss":{"f":"/f/ has a real contact, lip on teeth. This panel shows nothing touching in the mouth, with the only narrowing down at the vocal folds.","th":"/θ/ puts the tongue tip at the teeth. Here nothing in the mouth touches anything."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null}]},{"id":"wb","n":3,"title":"Word Builder","accent":"magenta","subtitle":"Build each word out of its sounds","concept":"Every spoken word is a chain of phonemes. Listen, then stamp the sounds into the boxes in order. The number of boxes tells you how many sounds the word has; your job is which sounds, in which order. This week several words start with a vowel, and one has more sounds than letters.","audio_note":"<b>Tap any tile to hear its sound.</b> Consonants are recorded with a vowel after, /b/ as <i>ba</i>, so listen to the front of the syllable.","prompt":"Tap the sounds in order to build the word. Tap a filled box to take one out.","tiles":[{"ipa":"b","slug":"b","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"p","slug":"p","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"t","slug":"t","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"k","slug":"k","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"g","slug":"g","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"f","slug":"f","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"s","slug":"s","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"l","slug":"l","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"n","slug":"n","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"h","slug":"h","cat":"c"},{"ipa":"ʌ","slug":"ah","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"i","slug":"iy","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"ɑʊ","slug":"aw","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"ɪ","slug":"ih","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"ɑ","slug":"aa","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"eɪ","slug":"ey","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"ʊ","slug":"uh","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"u","slug":"uw","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"ɛ","slug":"eh","cat":"v"},{"ipa":"oʊ","slug":"ow","cat":"v"}],"demo":{"word":"egg","audio":"w_egg","want":["eh","g"],"tiles":[{"ipa":"ɛ","slug":"eh"},{"ipa":"g","slug":"g"},{"ipa":"k","slug":"k"}],"cap":"<b>egg</b> has three letters but two sounds: no consonant in front, then one /g/ for the double <b>gg</b>. Tap the sounds in order. Tap a filled box to take it back out.","ok":"<b>/ɛ/ + /g/ = egg.</b> Two sounds, and the double <b>g</b> spells just one of them."},"items":[{"id":"wb01","type":"build","word":"up","audio":"w_up","answer":"ah.p","chips":[{"letters":"u","ipa":"ʌ","slug":"ah"},{"letters":"p","ipa":"p","slug":"p"}],"explain":"Two letters, two sounds. The <b>u</b> here is the short vowel /ʌ/ of <i>cup</i>, not the /u/ of <i>blue</i>.","miss":{"uh.p":"Close: /ʊ/ is the vowel of <i>put</i>. <i>Up</i> has the more open /ʌ/, the vowel of <i>cup</i>. Say <i>put</i>, then <i>up</i>, and hear the jaw drop."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb02","type":"build","word":"eat","audio":"w_eat","answer":"iy.t","chips":[{"letters":"ea","ipa":"i","slug":"iy"},{"letters":"t","ipa":"t","slug":"t"}],"explain":"Three letters, two sounds: the pair <b>ea</b> spells one vowel, /i/, and the word starts straight in on it with no consonant in front.","miss":{"ih.t":"/ɪ/ is the vowel of <i>it</i>. <i>Eat</i> is longer and tighter: /i/, the vowel of <i>see</i>. <i>It</i> and <i>eat</i> differ by exactly that vowel."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb03","type":"build","word":"owl","audio":"w_owl","answer":"aw.l","chips":[{"letters":"ow","ipa":"ɑʊ","slug":"aw"},{"letters":"l","ipa":"l","slug":"l"}],"explain":"Three letters, two sounds. <b>ow</b> spells the gliding vowel /ɑʊ/, one sound that starts open and rounds upward, and then /l/.","miss":{"ow.l":"The letters <b>ow</b> can spell /oʊ/, as in <i>low</i>, but in <i>owl</i> they spell the gliding vowel /ɑʊ/, as in <i>cow</i>. Trust your ear over the spelling."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb04","type":"build","word":"if","audio":"w_if","answer":"ih.f","chips":[{"letters":"i","ipa":"ɪ","slug":"ih"},{"letters":"f","ipa":"f","slug":"f"}],"explain":"Two letters, two sounds: the short vowel /ɪ/ and then /f/.","miss":{"iy.f":"/i/ is the long, tight vowel of <i>see</i>. <i>If</i> has the short, relaxed /ɪ/, the vowel of <i>it</i>."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb05","type":"build","word":"ox","audio":"w_ox","answer":"aa.k.s","chips":[{"letters":"o","ipa":"ɑ","slug":"aa"},{"letters":"x","ipa":"k","slug":"k","sub":"the x, first half"},{"letters":"","ipa":"s","slug":"s","sub":"the x, second half"}],"explain":"Two letters, <b>three</b> sounds. The single letter <b>x</b> spells two sounds in a row, /k/ and /s/, and the <b>o</b> is the open vowel /ɑ/ of <i>hot</i>.","miss":{"aa.k.k":"Only one /k/. The letter <b>x</b> is /k/ followed by /s/: say <i>ox</i> slowly and listen for the hiss at the end."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb06","type":"build","word":"baby","audio":"w_baby","answer":"b.ey.b.iy","chips":[{"letters":"b","ipa":"b","slug":"b"},{"letters":"a","ipa":"eɪ","slug":"ey"},{"letters":"b","ipa":"b","slug":"b"},{"letters":"y","ipa":"i","slug":"iy"}],"explain":"Four letters, four sounds, but the letters are not the sounds you might expect: the <b>a</b> is the gliding vowel /eɪ/, and the final <b>y</b> is the vowel /i/.","miss":{"b.ey.b.ih":"The last vowel of <i>baby</i> is closer to /i/, the vowel of <i>see</i>, than to /ɪ/. Say <i>baby</i> and stretch the ending: it is <i>ee</i>."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb07","type":"build","word":"honey","audio":"w_honey","answer":"h.ah.n.iy","chips":[{"letters":"h","ipa":"h","slug":"h"},{"letters":"o","ipa":"ʌ","slug":"ah"},{"letters":"n","ipa":"n","slug":"n"},{"letters":"ey","ipa":"i","slug":"iy"}],"explain":"Five letters, four sounds. The <b>o</b> is the /ʌ/ of <i>cup</i>, as in <i>money</i> and <i>son</i>, and the final <b>ey</b> spells the single vowel /i/.","miss":{"h.ow.n.iy":"The letter <b>o</b> usually tempts /oʊ/, but <i>honey</i> rhymes with <i>sunny</i>: the vowel is /ʌ/.","h.ah.n.ey":"The letters <b>ey</b> spell /eɪ/ in <i>they</i>, but at the end of <i>honey</i> they spell plain /i/. Listen to the ending: <i>ee</i>."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb08","type":"build","word":"sunny","audio":"w_sunny","answer":"s.ah.n.iy","chips":[{"letters":"s","ipa":"s","slug":"s"},{"letters":"u","ipa":"ʌ","slug":"ah"},{"letters":"nn","ipa":"n","slug":"n"},{"letters":"y","ipa":"i","slug":"iy"}],"explain":"Five letters, four sounds. The double <b>nn</b> is one /n/, and the final <b>y</b> is the vowel /i/. <i>Sunny</i> and <i>honey</i> rhyme exactly.","miss":{"s.uh.n.iy":"/ʊ/ is the vowel of <i>put</i>. <i>Sunny</i> has the open /ʌ/ of <i>sun</i>."},"also":[],"accept":{},"note":null},{"id":"wb09","type":"build","word":"cookie","audio":"w_cookie","answer":"k.uh.k.iy","chips":[{"letters":"c","ipa":"k","slug":"k"},{"letters":"oo","ipa":"ʊ","slug":"uh"},{"letters":"k","ipa":"k","slug":"k"},{"letters":"ie","ipa":"i","slug":"iy"}],"accept":{"k.uw.k.iy":"/u/ is the long, tight vowel of <i>food</i>. <i>Cookie</i> has the shorter, looser /ʊ/. Say <i>food</i>, then <i>cookie</i>, and feel the lips relax."},"explain":"Six letters, four sounds. <b>c</b> and <b>k</b> both spell /k/, <b>oo</b> is the short /ʊ/ of <i>book</i>, and <b>ie</b> spells the vowel /i/.","miss":{},"also":[],"note":null},{"id":"wb10","type":"build","word":"lego","audio":"w_lego","answer":"l.eh.g.ow","chips":[{"letters":"l","ipa":"l","slug":"l"},{"letters":"e","ipa":"ɛ","slug":"eh"},{"letters":"g","ipa":"g","slug":"g"},{"letters":"o","ipa":"oʊ","slug":"ow"}],"accept":{"l.ey.g.ow":"Some speakers do say <i>lay-go</i>, but the usual American pronunciation, and the one recorded here, has /ɛ/. Listen again for the first vowel."},"explain":"Four letters, four sounds. The <b>e</b> is the short /ɛ/ of <i>leg</i>, and the final <b>o</b> is the gliding vowel /oʊ/, as in <i>go</i>.","miss":{"l.eh.g.aa":"The final <b>o</b> is /oʊ/, the vowel of <i>go</i>, not the open /ɑ/ of <i>hot</i>."},"also":[],"note":null}]}]};
